    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadat-ali-khan-i">Saadat Ali Khan I</a>, appointed Subahdar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> in 1722, subdued local powers and consolidated Oudh as a state as Mughal authority declined.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> in 1764 began a long period of British interference, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> and cessions of land.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a> deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajid-ali-shah">Wajid Ali Shah</a> and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> on 7 February 1856, on the grounds of alleged misrule; Oudh rose against British rule in the rebellion of 1857.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi-ud-din-haidar-shah">Ghazi-ud-Din Haidar Shah</a> instituted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> Bequest, fixed payments by the British to the Shia holy cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a> and Karbala, paid as interest on a loan Oudh made to the Company in 1825.</p></aside>
    </section>

