    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a>, now obsolete, was once the anglicised name of the state, also written historically as Oudhe. Oudh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subah">Subah</a> was one of the original twelve subahs that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> established in his administrative reforms of 1572–1580, divided into sarkars and these into parganas. At its creation it consisted of five sarkars subdivided into 38 parganas. On 9 September 1722 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadat-ali-khan-i">Saadat Ali Khan I</a> was appointed Subahdar of Oudh Subah, succeeding Girdhar Bahadur. He at once subdued the autonomous Shaikhzadas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> and Raja Mohan Singh of Tiloi, consolidating Oudh as a state.</p><p>In 1728 Oudh acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a> and the surrounding lands from the Mughal noble Rustam Ali Khan, and established stable revenue collection there after quelling the chief of Azamgarh, Mahabat Khan. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> dissolved and governors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> asserted their autonomy, and as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> rose, the rulers of Oudh gradually affirmed their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. In 1740 Saadat Khan's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safdar-jang">Safdar Jang</a> moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> governed the fertile lands of the Central and Lower Doab and lay in a prosperous region; the affluence in which its nawabs lived drew the attention of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, and the Nawab of Oudh was counted among the richest princes. In the early eighteenth century its population was estimated at 3 million. Over the course of the century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> grew into metropolises of over 200,000 people at the expense of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. The land on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> suffered frequent dry spells, while the Baiswara did not.</p><p>Although Oudh was ruled by Muslims, roughly four fifths of its population were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>, Varanasi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> were important pilgrimage sites for followers of Hinduism and other Dharmic religions, and the town of Bahraich was revered by some Muslims. The Nawab paid for and erected a Residency in Lucknow as part of a wider programme of civic improvements. Feudatory estates, the taluqdaris or parganas, included Amethi, Balrampur, Mahmudabad, Pratapgarh, Nanpara and Tulsipur.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Oudh State&#8221; cites where it covers Demographics, Culture:</p><ul><li>J. R. I. Cole, Roots of North Indian Shīʾism in Iran and Iraq: Religion and State in Awadh, 1722-1859 (University of California Press, 1989)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saadat Khan mobilised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> in 1739 to defend against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shahs-invasion-of-india">Nader Shah's invasion of India</a> and was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karnal">Battle of Karnal</a>; he tried to negotiate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> but died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safdar-jang">Safdar Jang</a> continued an expansionist policy, promising military protection to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in exchange for the forts of Rohtasgarh and Chunar, and annexing portions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhabad">Farrukhabad</a>, ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-khan-bangash">Muhammad Khan Bangash</a>, with Mughal military aid.</p><p>The turning point came at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> in 1764, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> defeated the alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a> and the deposed nawab of Bengal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Kasim</a>. The British occupied the fort at Chunar, and Shuja-ud-Daula paid an indemnity of 5 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. With Company troops, paid for by Oudh, and Company aid in the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilla-war">Rohilla War</a>, Oudh gained the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> and the Middle Doab in 1774, leaving only Rampur State as a Rohilla enclave. Thereafter Oudh relied on British troops in its service, whose cost the Company repeatedly raised.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> came from a Shia Muslim Sayyid family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, descended from Musa al-Kadhim, and also traced descent on the paternal line to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-koyunlu">Kara Koyunlu</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a>; they were known for their secularism and broad outlook, and promoted Shia as the state religion. All used the title Nawab until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi-ud-din-haidar-shah">Ghazi-ud-Din Haidar Shah</a> took that of Badshah in 1819. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safdar-jang">Safdar Jang</a> controlled the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, placing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur">Ahmad Shah Bahadur</a> on the Mughal throne, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a> became vazir to Shah Alam II in 1762.</p><p>From 1773 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> appointed a resident, Nathaniel Middleton, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> to shape Oudh's policy and direct its internal affairs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-ud-daula">Asaf-ud-Daula</a> acceded with British aid, and from then on Oudh consistently complied with the Company's demands; his reliance on British aid made his rule essentially a puppet regime. Saadat Ali Khan II owed his throne in 1798 to Sir John Shore's proclamation in Lucknow. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> embassy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil">Vakil</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> also sat at the court until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> served above all as a buffer state protecting the frontiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and their trade there, and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> interests. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> of 1765 gave British traders special privileges and exemptions from many customs duties, which led to tension as British monopolies were established. In the Treaty of Benares of 1773 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a> bought the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kora">Kora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> from the British for 50 lakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>, a policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> that was unpopular among the rest of the Company's leadership.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> grew steadily. The Treaty of Benares of 1775 ceded the sarkars of Benares, Ghazipur, Chunar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a>; the Treaty of Chunar of 1781 failed to reduce the British troops in Oudh's service; and a treaty of 21 February 1798 raised the subsidy to 70 lakh rupees a year. Oudh's wealth also reached beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>: the Oudh Bequest, set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi-ud-din-haidar-shah">Ghazi-ud-Din Haidar Shah</a>, made fixed payments to the Shia holy cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a> and Karbala as interest on the Third Oudh Loan of 1825.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1801, under pressure from Lord Wellesley, Saadat Ali Khan II ceded the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a>, the Lower Doab and the sarkar of Gorakhpur in lieu of the annual tribute. The cession halved the state, left it surrounded by British territory and useless as a buffer, and bound it to a government serving its people; the British later cited failure to meet this demand to justify annexation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in May 1816, and further areas were ceded through the early 1800s.</p><p>On 7 February 1856 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a> deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajid-ali-shah">Wajid Ali Shah</a> and annexed Oudh under the Doctrine of Lapse on the grounds of alleged internal misrule. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>, from 5 July 1857 to 3 March 1858, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/begum-hazrat-mahal">Begum Hazrat Mahal</a> proclaimed her son Birjis Qadr Wali of Awadh and ruled as regent; the British reestablished their rule over the next eighteen months, and rebels fought on until 1859. Oudh was merged into the North-Western Provinces and Oudh, later the United Provinces, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in 1950.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> grew from a Mughal subah, consolidated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadat-ali-khan-i">Saadat Ali Khan I</a> from 1722, into a wealthy independent kingdom of the Doab with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> in 1764 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> drew ever more land and money from it, made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1816 and annexed it in 1856.</em></p>

